roles/template -- how a new seat sets itself up (about a minute)
================================================================================

You were told something like: "You're a new seat called <Name>. Use the
template in roles/template to set yourself up." This file is that template.
It is a FILE, not a directory, on purpose: the census, the comms roster and
the self-tests treat every directory under roles/ as a seat.

Do exactly this. Do not hand-write seat files; a tool writes and checks them.
Do not `git pull` (WORKING_CONTRACT.md s3). The canonical checkout may be
behind origin/main, so everything below reads from origin/main after a fetch.

0. Know your inputs.
   <Name>   the seat name the operator gave you, exactly as spelled.
   <model>  YOUR exact runtime model id, as your own instructions state it.
            Never guess it. If you cannot tell, ask the operator once.
   words    everything the operator said that created the seat, verbatim.

1. In the canonical checkout (the repository's main worktree) run:
       git fetch origin
   Nothing else is ever run there except the tool below, which only adds a
   worktree.

2. Save the operator's words, byte for byte, in a temporary file with your
   file-writing tool (not retyped from memory, not through a shell). Include
   the sentence that named you and any other words they said about the seat.

3. Check the name is free (about 8 seconds). Read-only; creates nothing:
       git show origin/main:roles/base-role/seat_kit/new_seat.py | python - <Name> --model <model> --dry-run
   If it says SEAT CREATION STOPPED ... already exists, STOP: tell the
   operator the name is taken (a seat that exists only on a branch counts) and
   suggest another. Do not continue under that name.

4. Create the seat (about 25-40 seconds):
       git show origin/main:roles/base-role/seat_kit/new_seat.py | python - <Name> --model <model> --directive-file <the file from step 2> --stated "<one thing the operator said about the seat, in their words>" --push
   Rules for the options:
     --stated      repeat once per distinct thing the operator said about the
                   seat (their adjectives, their purpose), quoted exactly.
                   Leave it out if they said nothing beyond the name.
     --host-label  add --host-label "<HOST (Mn)>" only if the operator named
                   the machine, e.g. "M2 (spectrex5)". Otherwise the tool uses
                   this machine's hostname.
     --trailer     if your session instructions give commit attribution lines
                   (Co-Authored-By, Claude-Session), pass each one as its own
                   --trailer "<line>". Omit if you have none.
     --push        publishes to origin/main as a fast-forward. The operator's
                   instruction to create the seat is the authority; omit
                   --push only if they told you not to publish.
   Needs: git, Python 3.8+, and psycopg2 for the comms boot. If psycopg2 is
   missing the seat is still created and the failed boot is recorded as a
   blocker; tell the operator. Do not install packages machine-wide.

5. The tool prints a receipt (seat, worktree path, branch, commit, comms boot,
   tests, push). Paste the receipt to the operator. Then STOP and say
   "Ready for your charter."

6. What you must NOT do, because you have no charter yet:
   - do not start any work, and do not interpret the operator's adjectives
     (for example "moonshot" or "self contained"): record them, never decide
     what they mean;
   - do not send a heartbeat to Aporia or post to comms unless told to;
   - do not create directories outside roles/<Name>/;
   - do not retry a failed run by hand-writing files, and do not edit another
     seat's files.

If the tool stops with a message, that message names the problem. Report it to
the operator with the text, and see roles/base-role/seat_kit/README.md
("Limits"). A failed validation keeps the worktree it names so it can be read.

Afterwards your seat's own wake block is roles/<Name>/WAKE.md. The charter,
when it arrives, goes in as roles/<Name>/prompts/<date>_charter/ with a
MANIFEST, and roles/<Name>/RESPONSIBILITIES.md is rewritten around it.

Source of this file: roles/base-role/seat_kit/ (templates, tool, controls).
Written 2026-10-04 at the operator's request. No base-role rule is changed.
